--- Finding (parse_an_ip_address): at the top level, a list literal holding a plain
--- int beside the answer of a program's function that can fail (and can answer an
--- int) is refused, in both modes, before the program runs, on the line that
--- assigns the variable for the first and only time:
---
---   parse_an_ip_address-1.co:23:1: cannot reassign 'zs' from array to
---   array[result[int]] — variable type cannot change
---     23 │ zs = [2, half[3]]
---
--- Expected: [2, error: line 20: 3 is odd] in both modes — a list literal may hold a
--- failed value (spec, for: "as a list literal holding a failed value is"), and
--- these all run in both modes: `[1, int['b'], 3]` at the top level (a builtin that
--- can fail), `[half[3], half[4]]` (every item can fail), and `[2, half[3]]` inside a
--- function. Not only-error values as in findings/monads_maybe_monad-1.co (whose
--- `xs = [1, nothing[]]` note shows the same words): `half` answers an int too.
--- (Found writing parse_an_ip_address; the solution does not need such a list.)

fn half[n]
    if n % 2 eq 1
        return error['`n` is odd', 'odd']
    return n / 2

zs = [2, half[3]]
log[zs]
